\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



A monic polynomial has leading coefficient one. In the covering convention used for lemniscates, circles'' means their enclosed closed discs: find discs $\overline B(z_j,r_j)$ with the sublevel set contained in their union and $\sum_jr_j\leq2$. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://www.erdosproblems.com/509}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $f(z)\in\mathbb{C}[z]$ be a monic non-constant polynomial. Can the set $ \{ z\in \mathbb{C} : \lvert f(z)\rvert \leq 1\} $ be covered by a set of circles the sum of whose radii is $\leq 2$? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Scope and interpretation.}

Literal circumferences cannot cover a lemniscate with interior in the intended finite-disc-covering sense. The disc interpretation is made explicit rather than silently substituting it. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Prove the sharp constant 2 or exhibit an obstruction. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can the unit sublevel set of every monic polynomial be covered by discs with total radii at most two?

